{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q014_paper_example_reclassification_pa1492","answer":"In SCOP 1.69, the PA1492 domain of 1t1j (d1t1ja_, sunid 106249) was classified as c.137.1.1, in its own fold c.137 ("Hypothetical protein PA1492", sunid 110459) and superfamily c.137.1 (sunid 110460). That placed it apart from nucleoside 2-deoxyribosyltransferase, which is in a different fold. In the current SCOPe release (2.08 as returned by this service), the corresponding domain d1t1ja1 (chain A residues 1-118) is c.23.14.2, family "Hypothetical protein PA1492" (sunid 110461), in superfamily c.23.14 "N-(deoxy)ribosyltransferase-like" (sunid 52309) within fold c.23 (Flavodoxin-like). The nucleoside 2-deoxyribosyltransferase domain d1f8ya_ (PDB 1f8y) is c.23.14.1, in the same superfamily 52309 and in family 52310 "N-deoxyribosyltransferase", and it carried the same superfamily in 1.69.

So the change is that PA1492 moved from a singleton fold (c.137) into the c.23.14 superfamily shared with 1f8y, becoming a sister family (c.23.14.2 vs c.23.14.1) rather than an unrelated fold. For 1f8y, the substantive classification did not change; only the superfamily label changed ("N-deoxyribosyltransferase" to "N-(deoxy)ribosyltransferase-like") and the species label format changed.

Limits: (1) A shared SCOP superfamily is a classification-level inference of common evolutionary origin. It does not establish the same function. PA1492 is annotated as a hypothetical structural-genomics protein of unknown function, so nothing here transfers nucleoside transferase activity to it. (2) SIDs and sunids are not comparable across releases. In 1.69 the 1t1j domain was the whole chain ("1t1j A:"), and the 2.08 SID d1t1ja1 covers residues 1-118, with a separate short segment d1t1ja2 (class l, residues 0-0) that I did not evaluate. Sunid 106249 and family sunid 110461 are reused across releases but with different parents, so the comparison must be made on lineage, not sunid. (3) The release 1.69 lookup for d1t1ja1 returned 404, which is why the 1.69 domain was taken from the 1t1j entry's domain list instead. (4) I could not retrieve the Andreeva et al. 2008 text through these tools, so I cannot confirm their exact wording or reasoning; the classification change described above is what the SCOPe data show. (5) I used the release reported as current (2.08 with a 2026-08-16 periodic update label); I did not separately check whether that periodic update altered these nodes.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info (/api/v1/releases/current)","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry (/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1t1j/domains, /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1f8y/domains, /api/v1/releases/1.69/pdb/1t1j/domains)","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains (/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1t1ja1/parents, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1f8ya_/parents, /api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1f8ya_/parents, /api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1t1ja_/parents, /api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1t1ja1 returned 404)","mcp__scope__search_scop (/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=PA1492; a 1.69 search attempt returned a 503 index error and was not relied on)"]}]}
